WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:10.039
What happened was I knew I was writing it and I remember that Rob Greenberg was in the room, the writer's room, and we were in there and we were like, it'll be great, it'll be great.
事情是这样的，我知道我在写那一集，我记得罗布·格林伯格也在编剧室里，我们当时都在那儿，觉得这太棒了，这太棒了。

00:00:10.039 --> 00:00:14.039
She'll write the wrong name and then a real call in that name all throughout the script.
她会写错名字，然后在整个剧本里所有人都真真切切地叫那个名字。

00:00:14.039 --> 00:00:25.070
And I either went to the bathroom or went to meet with you guys in your office for like one second and I came back in and Rob said, oh, we have a name that they could have written down.
然后要么是我去上厕所了，要么是去你们办公室找你们聊了一秒钟，当我走回来的时候，罗布说，哦，我们想到了一个他们可能会写下来的名字。

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:25.910
Okay, great.
好吧，太棒了。

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.070
Well, this is, he goes swarly.
这个名字，他说是“斯瓦利（Swarly）”。

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.690
And I said, you don't need to tell me anything else.
我说，你什么都不用多说了。

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.350
That's, I've got this.
这个，我搞定了。

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.779
I'm alone, E.I.
我孤身一人，E.I。

00:00:36.779 --> 00:00:39.979
won't be soon.
很快就不会孤单了。

00:00:39.979 --> 00:00:49.939
In New York City, when I see you, please permit me to tell you everything.
在纽约市，当我看到你时，请允许我告诉你一切。

00:00:49.939 --> 00:00:53.579
In New York City.
在纽约市。

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:56.740
Well, hello, welcome to another episode of How We Made Your Brother.
大家好，欢迎收听《老爸老妈的浪漫史》制作揭秘播客。

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:57.579
I'm Josh Radner.
我是乔什·拉德纳。

00:00:57.579 --> 00:00:58.780
I'm here with my friend Craig Thomas.
我和我的朋友克雷格·托马斯在这里。

00:00:58.780 --> 00:00:59.780
Hello, Craig.
你好，克雷格。

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:00.780
Hey, Josh.
嘿，乔什。

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.099
Craig co-created a show with Carter Bayes called How I Met Your Mother.
克雷格和卡特·贝斯共同创作了一部名为《老爸老妈的浪漫史》的剧集。

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:09.060
I started on that show as Ted Mosby from 2005 to 2014.
我从2005年到2014年在这部剧中饰演泰德·莫斯布。

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.180
We did 208 episodes of the show.
我们一共拍了208集。

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.900
We're making our way through them one by one.
我们现在正一集一集地重温它们。

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:19.819
We also talk a lot about what went into every decision.
我们也会深入探讨每一个决定背后的故事。

00:01:19.819 --> 00:01:21.739
We're getting pretty granular.
我们讲得相当细致。

00:01:21.739 --> 00:01:28.420
Craig and Carter, of course, were joined by a murderer's row of fantastic writers.
当然，克雷格和卡特身边有一群顶尖的优秀编剧。

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.420
And we have one of the best here with us today.
今天我们非常荣幸地请到了其中一位佼佼者。

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.859
Craig say a little bit more to tee it up.
克雷格，请你再多介绍一点来开个场吧。

00:01:33.859 --> 00:01:34.859
Oh, yes.
哦，好的。

00:01:34.859 --> 00:01:49.060
This gentleman joined us in season two with a very intimidating resume joining me and Carter who had never even been on staff on a multi-camera sitcom, technically speaking, and Greg joined us and I have already blown the name.
这位先生在第二季加入了我们，带着一份极其吓人的履历加入了我和卡特——从严格意义上讲，我和卡特之前甚至从未在多机位情景喜剧的编剧团队里待过，然后格雷格加入了我们，而我已经把名字给说错了。

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.099
Ladies and gentlemen, it's Greg Maylands, let's just get right into it.
女士们先生们，这位是格雷格·梅伦茨，我们直接切入正题吧。

00:01:52.099 --> 00:01:58.666
Greg Maylands, who worked on so many great shows, including Friends and co-running or running friends for several years.
格雷格·梅伦茨，他参与过许多优秀的剧集，包括《老友记》，并且担任过《老友记》的联合制片人或制片人好几年。

00:01:58.666 --> 00:02:09.391
I mean, Greg was a name we would see when Alec, I went to college with Alec, we would watch friends in college and like see Greg's name on there who was very young writing for friends at the time.
我的意思是，格雷格这个名字我们以前经常看到，当年亚历克——我和亚历克上大学时，我们在大学里看《老友记》，会看到格雷格的名字出现在上面，当时他年纪轻轻就在为《老友记》写剧本了。

00:02:09.391 --> 00:02:17.379
And we'd, I would think to myself someday, maybe I'll just at least get to meet maybe a guy like Greg Maylands and just like talk to him one time.
我们会，我当时心里会想，也许有一天，我至少能有机会见见像格雷格·梅伦茨这样的人，跟他聊上一回。

00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:27.219
Instead, Greg joined season two of how much mother and did multiple seasons helping us as incredibly valuable and wonderful and all, not just a great writer, but just a great dude.
结果呢，格雷格加入了《老爸老妈的浪漫史》第二季，参与制作了多个季度，不仅提供了难以置信的宝贵和出色的帮助，而且不仅是个伟大的编剧，还是个绝佳的伙计。

00:02:27.219 --> 00:02:29.528
Let's, let's keep complimenting him for 45 minutes.
让我们，让我们继续夸他45分钟吧。

00:02:29.528 --> 00:02:30.659
I don't want him to talk.
我不想让他说话。

00:02:30.659 --> 00:02:33.780
I want to throw, I want to throw one more thing out there about that.
我想再抛出一个关于这方面的事情。

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.294
This is Greg, by the way, acclamate to his voice.
顺便说一下，这位就是格雷格，大家熟悉一下他的声音。

00:02:37.294 --> 00:02:37.939
Go ahead.
请说吧。

00:02:37.939 --> 00:02:46.159
The preamble to working on the show was I was on Will and Grace the year before, when you guys did your first season of Will and Grace.
加入这部剧的序曲是，前一年我曾在《威尔和格蕾丝》剧组，当时你们正在制作《威尔和格蕾丝》的第一季。

00:02:46.159 --> 00:03:10.025
And there were three writers on Will and Grace, and we were all, you know, it was a cliche, I used to have put, there were three of us who were watching how I met your mother from the first episode all the way through and we'd come in the next day and the writers would talk about the episodes and stuff and those and how much we loved the show and this was the last season of Will and Grace.
《威尔和格蕾丝》当时有三个编剧，我们都在，你知道，这有点像个老套的说法，以前有三个我们这样的人，从第一集开始一直追看《老爸老妈的浪漫史》，第二天我们就会来到办公室，编剧们会讨论那些剧集什么的，以及我们是多么喜爱这部剧，而那正好是《威尔和格蕾丝》的最后一季。

00:03:10.025 --> 00:03:23.419
So I was already like looking for what could be next for me but it was Jamie Ronheimer, Robbie Rashid and me all on Will and Grace talking about how I met your mother, all of us ended up writing on how I met your mother.
所以我当时已经在寻找我的下一站了，当时在《威尔和格蕾丝》剧组讨论《老爸老妈的浪漫史》的有杰米·罗恩海默、罗比·拉希德和我，我们所有人最后都加入了《老爸老妈的浪漫史》的编剧团队。

00:03:23.419 --> 00:03:24.620
That's so cool.
太酷了。

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.099
For multiple seasons, I forgot that little trivia fact.
合作了多个季度，我都快把这个小八卦给忘了。

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:27.599
Yeah.
是啊。

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:28.675
That's amazing.
太神奇了。

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:40.793
And Greg, you kind of sought us out a little bit and made us look very good to our bosses because you were a way bigger name than we were and we were not a hit show yet.
而且格雷格，你当时可以说是主动来找我们的，这让我们在老板面前显得很有面子，因为你的名气比我们大得多，而我们当时还不是一部大热剧。

00:03:40.793 --> 00:03:42.084
It was a big show.
那是一部大剧。

00:03:42.084 --> 00:03:42.801
It wasn't.
并不是。

00:03:42.801 --> 00:03:43.589
It was not.
真的不是。

00:03:43.589 --> 00:03:46.601
It was season one was okay and it did not.
第一季表现还行，但并没有……

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:57.669
I love the show, the biggest thing for me was if I could write on this show I would learn so much.
我喜欢这部剧，对我来说最棒的一点是，如果我能为这部剧写剧本，我能学到很多东西。

00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:00.005
It was a show like I've never seen.
这是一部我从未见过的剧集。

00:04:00.005 --> 00:04:09.750
The way you guys created it essentially in television, how to tell a story in a very, very different way than it had ever been done on television.
你们在电视界开创这种叙事方式的方式，如何以一种与以往电视上完全不同的方式来讲故事。

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:18.499
I mean, on Friends, we did seven flashbacks maybe, 10 years, but I was like, it seemed like I could learn so much from you guys.
我的意思是，在《老友记》里，10年的时间里我们大概只用了7次闪回，但我觉得我可以从你们身上学到很多东西。

00:04:18.499 --> 00:04:24.857
And that's why I pursued it because I didn't have, you know, I was open and I loved the show.
这就是我追求它的原因，因为我没有什么，你知道的，我很开放而且我很喜欢这部剧。

00:04:24.857 --> 00:04:27.660
So it seemed like it made a lot of sense.
所以这看起来非常有道理。

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:39.335
Greg, given that you, you had such a history before how I met my mother writing on more kind of classically structured sitcoms, did you, before you saw how I met your mother, Did you feel narratively kind of hemmed in?
格雷格，鉴于你在加入《老爸老妈的浪漫史》之前，有着在那种结构更经典的情景喜剧中写作的丰富历史，在你看到《老爸老妈的浪漫史》之前，你在叙事上有没有感到受到限制？

00:04:39.335 --> 00:04:44.369
Like, or did how I make your mother show you that there was more possible to do with the form?
或者说，《老爸老妈的浪漫史》是否向你展示了这种形式还有更多的可能性？

00:04:44.369 --> 00:04:46.779
The second one, definitely not the first one.
是第二种情况，绝对不是第一种。

00:04:46.779 --> 00:04:47.279
Right.
没错。

00:04:47.279 --> 00:04:54.714
Yeah, but look, we talked about this off air, but it goes back to my desire to not stagnate.
是的，但听着，我们在节目之外聊过这个，这要追溯到我不想停滞不前的愿望。

00:04:54.714 --> 00:05:07.564
And if I could learn and grow and discover a whole new way of writing, which I'll be totally honest, I'm not sure I ever got as good as you guys at doing that.
如果我能学习、成长并发现一种全新的写作方式——说句实话，我不确定我在这方面的水平是否能达到你们的高度。

00:05:07.564 --> 00:05:08.776
But I got good.
但我变强了。

00:05:08.776 --> 00:05:10.635
Like I was much better.
就像我进步了很多。

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:11.792
Do you know what I love?
你知道我最喜欢什么吗？

00:05:11.792 --> 00:05:14.527
One of the many things I love about you, I'll keep calm.
我喜欢你的诸多优点之一，我会保持冷静。

00:05:14.527 --> 00:05:17.067
I'm just going to compliment you for the whole hour.
我接下来的整整一个小时都要用来称赞你。

00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:22.879
You could have come in, and by the way, the network and the studio probably would have loved you coming in saying this.
你当时完全可以走进来——顺便说一句，电视网和制片公司可能会非常乐意听到你这么说。

00:05:22.879 --> 00:05:26.396
You could have come in and said, let's make this even more like friends.
你本可以走进来然后说，让我们把这部剧变得更像《老友记》吧。

00:05:26.396 --> 00:05:27.519
Friends was a huge hit.
《老友记》是一部巨大的成功。

00:05:27.519 --> 00:05:29.180
Let's make this more like friends.
让我们把它变得更像《老友记》。

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:43.206
You came in instead and didn't see all the similarities of how much a mother to friends, which there are many and we love from, like I said, Alec, our producer and I watched friends together in our college living room and our house we lived in every week.
但你进来的时候，却没有把《老爸老妈的浪漫史》和《老友记》看作一模一样（尽管它们有很多相似之处，我们也都很喜欢，就像我说的，我们的制片人亚历克和我在大学的客厅里、在我们住的房子里每周一起看《老友记》。

00:05:43.206 --> 00:05:49.586
We love friends with all respect, but like we didn't want how I met your mother to just, and try to mimic that show.
出于所有的尊重，我们深爱《老友记》，但我们不希望《老爸老妈的浪漫史》只是去模仿那部剧。

00:05:49.586 --> 00:05:51.402
We wanted it to be its own thing.
我们希望它能走自己的路，成为它自己。

00:05:51.402 --> 00:05:57.837
You came in and really saw what was different about how I met your mother and said, do more of that, do more of that.
你进来之后，真的看出了《老爸老妈的浪漫史》与众不同的地方，并说：多搞点这个，多搞点这个。

00:05:57.837 --> 00:06:02.953
And it kind of, watching this episode, Swarley, that's the episode we're talking about today.
看着这一集——《斯瓦利》，这就是我们今天要聊的一集。

00:06:02.953 --> 00:06:04.108
They're very classic.
它们非常经典。

00:06:04.108 --> 00:06:07.353
Greg's first of several episodes he wrote and an all-timer.
这是格雷格编写的几集剧集中的第一集，也是神作级别的一集。

00:06:07.353 --> 00:06:13.789
And I love how this episode begins with a cold open that very much differentiates friends from how I met your mother.
我喜欢这一集的开场白，一个冷开场，它非常明确地将《老友记》与《老爸老妈的浪漫史》区分开来。

00:06:13.789 --> 00:06:17.694
And it was sort of, I felt that that encapsulated your entire approach.
某种程度上，我觉得这概括了你的整体方法。

00:06:17.694 --> 00:06:21.159
You said, no, be your own thing, and I want to be part of that.
你说，不，要走你自己的路，而我想成为其中的一部分。

00:06:21.159 --> 00:06:21.819
I love that.
我太喜欢这样了。

00:06:21.819 --> 00:06:24.459
Thank you for that coming in with that attitude.
感谢你带着这种态度加入我们。

00:06:24.459 --> 00:06:40.147
I got to talk about that for a second, because I did not write it in that way at all, like I just wrote it, but it seemed like they usually drink beer and in this scene they had to, because of the wrong name on the coffee cup, they had to be in a coffee shop.
我得稍微聊一下这个，因为我写它的时候根本不是那个意思，我只是顺着写了下去，但看起来他们通常是喝啤酒的，而在这个场景中，因为咖啡杯上的名字写错了，他们必须待在一家咖啡馆里。

00:06:40.147 --> 00:06:42.752
What's a good joke that can come from that?
有什么好笑的梗能从这里生发出来呢？

00:06:42.752 --> 00:06:47.477
And I just thought saying, well, drinking beer is better than drinking coffee.
我当时只是觉得，说“喝啤酒比喝咖啡好”挺有意思的。

00:06:47.477 --> 00:06:49.415
It wasn't even thinking of that.
我当时根本没想过那个。

00:06:49.415 --> 00:06:55.351
I forgot about that because we had a lot of the same crew as friends did on how I met your mother.
我都忘了这一点了，因为《老爸老妈的浪漫史》和《老友记》用的是很多相同的剧组人员。

00:06:55.351 --> 00:07:04.134
And I got down onto the stage that first day and a couple of the cameramen who I knew from friends were like, oh, that was a good dig at friends.
第一天我来到片场，几个我从《老友记》时期就认识的摄像师就说，哦，那是对《老友记》的一次绝妙嘲讽啊。

00:07:04.134 --> 00:07:06.193
This is a good dig at the friends.
这是对《老友记》的一个好讽刺。

00:07:06.193 --> 00:07:08.677
And I was like, wait, what is a good dig?
当时我就想，等等，什么好讽刺？

00:07:08.677 --> 00:07:12.190
And they pointed it out and I was like, oh, yeah, totally.
然后他们指了出来，我就想，哦，对啊，完全是这样。

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:14.310
I can see why you would think that.
我能理解你们为什么会这么想。

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.066
But it didn't come from that.
但它并不是从那儿来的。

00:07:16.066 --> 00:07:20.003
I think it's pretty universally acknowledged as a dig at friends.
我觉得这几乎被普遍认为是对《老友记》的嘲讽。

00:07:20.003 --> 00:07:44.716
Like, I don't think anyone thinks of it that it's not that all I can tell you is I wasn't there might have been some you were subconsciously like Freudian like killing your father or something like you were yeah I know I love in this episode though that it starts with a friends joke and ends with a cheers joke yeah and there's something so great about that that you know in some ways we were a humble show.
我觉得没有人会觉得它不是——我能告诉你的是，我当时并没有，也许你潜意识里有点像弗洛伊德式的“弑父”情结之类的，你当时……是的，我知道，不过我真的很喜欢这一集以一个《老友记》的笑话开场，并以一个《干杯酒吧》的笑话结尾，是的，这里面有一种非常美妙的东西，你知道的，在某种程度上我们曾是一部卑微的剧集。

00:07:44.716 --> 00:07:46.776
We were trying to stay on the air.
我们当时在努力争取留在电视上。

00:07:46.776 --> 00:07:53.802
We were, but also, I think we were really trying to situate ourselves in the constellation of great classic sitcoms.
我们是，但同时，我认为我们真的在努力将自己定位在伟大经典情景喜剧的星图之中。

00:07:53.802 --> 00:07:54.302
Yeah.
是的。

00:07:54.302 --> 00:07:54.802
Yeah.
没错。

00:07:54.802 --> 00:08:02.737
And even the, we can get there later, but the ending with the Cheers font is so funny and such a little wink towards sitcom lovers.
甚至还有，我们稍后可以聊到这个，片尾用的是《干杯酒吧》的字体，这太有趣了，也是对情景喜剧爱好者的一次小小致意。

00:08:02.737 --> 00:08:03.948
It made me so happy.
这让我太开心了。

00:08:03.948 --> 00:08:07.825
And your name's in there because Greg was an executive producer.
而且你的名字也在上面，因为格雷格是执行制片人。

00:08:07.825 --> 00:08:10.430
Greg's name is up there in the Cheers logo.
格雷格的名字就印在《老爸老妈浪漫史》的标志里。

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:14.064
That made me happier than in anything in the entire episode.
这让我比这一集里的任何事情都高兴。

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:16.670
We'll seeing that before we go on.
我们继续之前会看到那个。

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:22.790
Let's just say this episode is called Swarly and Alec, when did this episode air?
我们先说这一集叫《斯瓦利》，阿莱克，这一集是什么时候播出的？

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:25.350
December 8th, 2006.
2006年12月8日。

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.389
December 8th, 2006.
2006年12月8日。

00:08:27.389 --> 00:08:33.190
And Craig, or maybe even Greg, you can give like a little summation of what is this episode.
克雷格，或者甚至是格雷格，你能简单总结一下这一集讲的是什么吗？

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:34.029
What happens?
发生了什么？

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:37.470
Clicky log line for people who didn't just watch it the way that we'd just watch it.
给那些刚才没像我们这样重温这一集的观众做个简短精炼的剧情介绍。

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:40.803
It was a really big episode impactful important.
这是一集非常重大、极具冲击力和重要意义的剧集。

00:08:40.803 --> 00:08:50.665
And I remember fighting to be able to write it because I really wanted to write it, because Marshall and Lily have been broken up for so long.
我记得我曾极力争取来写这一集，因为我真的很想写，而且马歇尔和莉莉已经分手很久了。

00:08:50.665 --> 00:08:55.387
And this was the episode where they were going to get back together.
这一集讲的就是他们将要复合。

00:08:55.387 --> 00:08:58.582
And that one I really, really wanted to write.
所以我真的、真的很想写那一集。

00:08:58.582 --> 00:09:02.610
And I remember fighting for it and was granted the script.
我记得我为此争取过，最后拿到了剧本。

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:05.180
And so I was really excited about it.
所以我对此真的非常兴奋。

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:11.569
And that was a great storyline in it and the big getting back together of Marshall and Lily.
那里面有一条很棒的故事线，就是马歇尔和莉莉的盛大复合。

00:09:11.569 --> 00:09:14.625
But the other storyline overtook everything.
但另一条故事线盖过了所有风头。

00:09:14.625 --> 00:09:15.597
It really did.
确实如此。

00:09:15.597 --> 00:09:23.028
And became the title of the show, which was in a coffee shop that a coffee girl misspells Barney as swarly.
并且成了这集剧名的由来，也就是在一家咖啡馆里，一个咖啡店女孩把巴尼的名字拼错了，叫成了斯瓦利。

00:09:23.028 --> 00:09:26.917
And that was what happened was, I knew I was writing it.
当时的情况是，我知道我要写这一集。

00:09:26.917 --> 00:09:31.710
And I remember that Rob Greenberg was in the room, the writer's room.
我记得罗布·格林伯格当时也在编剧室里。

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:34.900
And we were in there and we were like, that'll be great.
我们当时在那儿讨论，觉得这主意太棒了。

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:57.237
You'll write the wrong name and that a real calling that name all throughout the script and we were breaking the episode all down with the writing staff and I knew I was writing it, they knew I was writing it and I either went to the bathroom or went to meet with you guys in your office for like one second and I came back in and Rob said oh we have a name that they could have written down.
你写错名字，然后在整个剧本里大家都叫这个错名字，我们当时正和编剧团队一起梳理这一集的剧情，我知道我要写，他们也知道我要写，然后我要么是去上个厕所，要么是去你们办公室找你们聊了一秒钟，等我回到房间里，罗布就说，哦，我们想出了一个他们可以写下来的名字。

00:09:57.237 --> 00:10:03.789
I go okay great what is it and he goes swarly and I said you don't need to tell me anything else but I've got this.
我说，行啊，太好了，是什么？他说是斯瓦利，我说，你什么都不用多说了，这个我接了。

00:10:03.789 --> 00:10:05.789
You made the sale, so stop selling it.
你已经把创意卖出去了，就别再推销了。

00:10:05.789 --> 00:10:07.590
I need nothing else.
我什么都不需要了。

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:11.990
I have this all through my head, everything started swirling, as it did for everybody.
我脑子里全是这个，所有思绪开始旋转，就像大家的反应一样。

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:12.490
Swirling.
旋转。

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:12.990
Swirling.
旋转。

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:13.490
Swirling.
旋转。

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:14.247
Swirling sparkly.
旋转，闪闪发光。

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:14.450
Yeah.
对。

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:15.394
Like every like.
就像每个都。

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:16.301
Oh, my God.
哦，天哪。

00:10:16.301 --> 00:10:19.029
So I texted Rob about this.
所以我为此给罗布发了条信息。

00:10:19.029 --> 00:10:20.710
And I said, you came up with the name Swarley, right?
我说，斯瓦利这个名字是你想出来的，对吧？

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:22.190
Because I remember that too, Greg.
因为我也记得是这样，格雷格。

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:27.389
And Rob wrote back, yes, I use my propensity for verbal gaps for infamy.
然后罗布回信说，是的，我把我老是说错话的毛病用来遗臭万年了。

00:10:28.389 --> 00:10:31.070
I think he will often say words and names the wrong way.
我觉得他经常会把词语和名字念错。

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.509
And this just he had a natural talent.
这只是他有一种天赋。

00:10:33.789 --> 00:10:34.629
to come up with it.
能想出这个。

00:10:34.629 --> 00:10:39.389
It's also one of those perfect things because it's not like carny.
这也是那种绝妙的点子之一，因为它不像“嘉年华艺人（carny）”。

00:10:39.389 --> 00:10:42.830
It's not like, it's like close, but it's not that close.
它不像，感觉挺接近，但又没那么近。

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:43.830
Like it's just weird.
就觉得很怪。

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:44.830
It's wonderfully not obvious.
它妙就妙在不那么显眼。

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:45.669
It's just weird enough.
怪得刚刚好。

00:10:45.669 --> 00:10:47.909
It's ridiculously not that close.
离得离谱地远。

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:53.309
It shares an R sound and an E ending.
它只有一个R的音和一个E的结尾。

00:10:53.309 --> 00:10:54.149
That's it.
就这些。

00:10:54.149 --> 00:10:59.990
But how great of Rob, by the way, for me to walk back into that room and say we came up with a good name.
不过罗布真厉害，话说回来，我走进那个房间说我们想出一个好名字。

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:00.830
Yeah.
对。

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:04.230
Without saying, I came up with a good name.
却没有说，这是我想出来的名字。

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:05.909
Yeah, he didn't say credit.
对，他没有居功。

00:11:05.909 --> 00:11:10.470
No, I walked in and he said, we came up with, yeah, he didn't say credit, which is great.
不，我走进去，他说，我们想出了个，对，他没有抢功，这太棒了。

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:20.509
And I wrote you this little day to sing to you in New York City.
我为你写了这首小曲，要在纽约市唱给你听。

00:11:20.509 --> 00:11:25.330
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:28.450
I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed Podcast.
我是亚历克斯·戈德曼，《超矫枉过正》播客的主持人。

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:31.970
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们收集听众的烦恼并试着帮他们解决。

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:34.847
problems like I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如这样的烦恼：我30岁了，但不敢在纽约开车。

00:11:34.847 --> 00:11:38.381
Or why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者为什么我在倒车时不能调节汽车音响的音量？

00:11:38.381 --> 00:11:40.350
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决和汽车无关的问题。

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:46.610
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that caused that problem in the first place.
如果你有什么烦恼，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭开导致这个烦恼的幕后隐藏机制。

00:11:47.389 --> 00:11:52.273
That's the Hyper-Fixed podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的《超矫枉过正》播客。

00:11:52.273 --> 00:11:58.506
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at HyperFixed Pod.com.
你可以在各大播客平台或HyperFixedPod.com找到它。

00:11:58.506 --> 00:12:01.208
And now, back to the show.
现在，回到节目中。

00:12:01.208 --> 00:12:08.377
There's one guy, there's a classic, well, it's not about the episode.
有一个家伙，有个经典桥段，好吧，其实跟这集没关系。

00:12:08.377 --> 00:12:17.519
But there's a classic how I met your mother's story, a joke, that was never in the show.
但有一个关于《老爸老妈浪漫史》的经典故事、一个笑话，从未在剧集里出现过。

00:12:17.519 --> 00:12:23.138
It was just a room bit that there's still a huge debate about, which we can get to later, I can do it right now.
那只是编剧室里的一个梗，大家到现在还在激烈争论，我们待会儿可以聊，我现在就能说。

00:12:23.138 --> 00:12:24.843
No, you have to tell us right now.
不，你现在就得告诉我们。

00:12:24.843 --> 00:12:26.799
I don't know what you're talking about.
我不知道你在说什么。

00:12:26.799 --> 00:12:36.651
I had come up with something that I thought was funny, and I think it was for some storyline that we never did, or maybe you did it after I left.
我当时想出了一个我觉得很好笑的点子，我想那可能是为了某个我们从未拍过的剧情线，或者也许是我离开后你们拍了。

00:12:36.651 --> 00:13:11.099
I don't really remember, but I just thought it was funny and it was a it was a novel and it was called revengeance and I thought that was funny because it was about a guy who got revenge but then needed to get it again revengeance and that to me was funny enough but then and this is where the big thing is my recollection luckily I'm on this and Stephen Lloyd is not Stephen Lloyd said the tagline to that book is this time it's personal again Which I thought was one of the funniest lines I've ever heard.
我其实记不清了，但我就是觉得它很好笑，那是一部小说，书名叫《复仇续集》（Revengeance），我觉得这名字很搞笑，因为讲的是一个家伙报了仇之后，发现还需要再报一次仇，也就是“复仇续集”，对我来说这已经够好笑了，然后——这就是最绝的地方——根据我的记忆，幸好有我在，而斯蒂芬·劳埃德不在——斯蒂芬·劳埃德说那本书的宣传标语是：“这次，又是私人恩怨。”我觉得这是我听过的最好笑的台词之一。

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:13.100
How do we not find a place for that?
我们怎么就没找到地方用上它呢？

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:17.559
To this day, Stephen Lloyd swears that I came up with it, and he'll tell me that.
直到今天，斯蒂芬·劳埃德还坚信这个点子是他想出来的，他一直这么跟我说。

00:13:17.559 --> 00:13:18.559
Wow.
哇。

00:13:18.559 --> 00:13:28.240
And I was, even at the strike, somebody came up to one of the writers I used to work with and said, oh, you know me telling you a joke in the room all the time, on whatever show he was working on.
而且我，甚至在罢工期间，有人走到一个跟我合作过的编剧面前说，哦，你知道我老是在他参与的某个剧组的编剧室里跟你说的一个笑话吗。

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:29.240
I said, what joke is that?
我说，什么笑话？

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:30.240
And he goes, this time it's personal again.
他说：“这次，又是私人恩怨。”

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:32.759
And I was like, that wasn't me.
当时我就想，那不是我说的。

00:13:32.759 --> 00:13:42.759
Stephen Lloyd said it was you are a member and I said Stephen, if I had come up with a joke that funny, I would never have forgotten it, but I can promise you that.
斯蒂芬·劳埃德说那是你想的，我说，斯蒂芬，如果我想出了这么好笑的笑话，我绝对不会忘，我可以向你保证这一点。

00:13:42.759 --> 00:13:48.759
Oh my God, that is so Stephen Lloyd, that's a good portrait of a good writer's room where people are fighting to not take credit for a joke.
哦，天哪，这太符合斯蒂芬·劳埃德的作风了，这真是一间优秀编剧室的生动写照：大家都在争着不肯为一个笑话居功。

00:13:48.759 --> 00:13:50.759
You know it's good people in that room.
你知道那个房间里都是优秀的人。

00:13:50.759 --> 00:13:57.759
Yeah, well it's also a metaphor for the show itself, which is like the fallibility of memory and like who did what when you know.
对，这也成了这部剧本身的隐喻，比如记忆的不可靠性，以及到底谁在什么时候做了什么，你知道的。

00:13:57.759 --> 00:13:59.759
That's a really good point.
这真的是个很好的观点。

00:13:59.759 --> 00:14:05.759
But anyway, that's the sub-slide trick of that joke, but I still find so funny.
不管怎样，那个笑话有种潜台词般的恶作剧效果，但我现在依然觉得很好笑。

00:14:05.759 --> 00:14:11.759
Anytime I start something with this time and end it with again, it's going to be fun.
每当我用“这次”开头、用“又来”结尾来开启一件事情时，都会很有趣。

00:14:11.759 --> 00:14:14.759
I can't believe we can't and didn't find a way to get that.
我简直不敢相信我们居然没能也没找到办法把这个用进去。

00:14:14.759 --> 00:14:21.759
Craig, when they pay us the mountains of money to do the movie, the one-off movie, we gotta get that in there.
克雷格，等他们付给我们成堆的钱去拍那部电影，那部独立电影时，我们一定要把它塞进去。

00:14:21.759 --> 00:14:23.759
You have to make it right and get that in there.
你必须得补上，把它放进去。

00:14:23.759 --> 00:14:27.759
I feel like Marshall gets bored of being a lawyer and he writes crime novels on the side.
我觉得马歇尔当律师当腻了，私底下开始写犯罪小说。

00:14:27.759 --> 00:14:28.259
the side.
私底下。

00:14:28.259 --> 00:14:28.874
Revengeance.
《复仇续集》。

00:14:28.874 --> 00:14:29.385
Oh my God.
哦，天哪。

00:14:29.385 --> 00:14:38.137
Greg, I completely forgot and still don't fully believe that you didn't mean that friend's reference because him, him fans excerpt that cold open and play it all the time.
格雷格，我完全忘了，而且依然不太相信你刚才不是在玩《老友记》的梗，因为粉丝们总是截取那个开场白并一直拿来播放。

00:14:38.137 --> 00:14:40.031
I think at least it was subconscious.
我觉得至少那是潜意识里的。

00:14:40.031 --> 00:14:48.271
I'm going with Josh's theory that subconsciously you knew because I'm so convinced that you meant it and that you've told me this before that you didn't mean it.
我赞同乔什的理论，觉得你潜意识里是知道的，因为我太确信你是那个意思了，而且你以前也跟我说过你不是那个意思。

00:14:48.271 --> 00:14:51.188
But I just my brain doesn't let it in and watch it again.
但我脑子就是接受不了，再看一遍时。

00:14:51.188 --> 00:14:53.082
I was like, all right, Greg did that.
我当时就想，好吧，格雷格确实这么干了。

00:14:53.082 --> 00:14:55.692
I will never fully believe that you didn't mean it.
我永远无法完全相信你不是故意的。

00:14:55.692 --> 00:14:57.279
I'm just saying that right now.
我只是随口这么一说。

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:58.240
Well, you know what?
好吧，你知道吗？

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:03.240
It's like a song, like, well, whatever lyric you hear that means something to you, that's great.
这就像是一首歌，不管你听到什么歌词对你有什么意义，那都很好。

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:04.240
I'm fine with that.
我对此没意见。

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:09.879
But I had no, I certainly came into having my mother having no animosity towards friends, whatsoever.
但我当时对《老友记》绝对、百分之百没有任何敌意。

00:15:09.879 --> 00:15:10.879
No, no.
不，不。

00:15:10.879 --> 00:15:11.879
And neither do we.
我们也是。

00:15:11.879 --> 00:15:14.240
I want to make that very, no one, no one did, yeah, great.
我想明确这一点，没人，真的没人，对，太棒了。

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:24.720
Because sometimes people excerpt that and they go, ah, friends, him, him took the piss out of friends and it wasn't, even if it had been over the friends' reference, it wouldn't have been that because we adored friends.
因为有时人们会断章取义，然后说，啊，老友记，他拿老友记开涮了，其实根本不是这样，即便真的是致敬老友记，也不会是那种恶意，因为我们太喜欢《老友记》了。

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:26.559
he wanted to write a show because of friends.
他就是因为《老友记》才想写一部剧的。

00:15:26.559 --> 00:15:31.080
All they really showed was that our protagonists were alcoholics, it was kind of a cell phone.
他们真正展现的其实是我们的主角都是酒鬼，这有点像手机。

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:32.639
Yeah, it was a cell phone.
对，就像个手机。

00:15:32.639 --> 00:15:34.200
But it also seemed true.
但这也显得很真实。

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:35.679
Yeah, it is true.
对，确实很真实。

00:15:35.679 --> 00:15:37.519
Yeah, it is true.
对，确实如此。

00:15:37.519 --> 00:15:38.360
In their case, it's true.
对他们而言，这是真的。

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:44.519
There's also more potential for dramatic chaos to unfold at night at a bar versus day time in a coffee shop.
而且相比于白天在咖啡馆，晚上在酒吧里更容易发生戏剧性的混乱。

00:15:44.519 --> 00:15:46.240
You're telling me.
可不是嘛。

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:51.240
Yeah, it's a credit to friends that they got that many stories out of hanging out at a coffee shop.
对，能在咖啡馆闲聊里榨取出那么多故事，真得归功于《老友记》。

00:15:51.240 --> 00:16:07.039
Well, just to ask, I feel like I have ask the very obvious question, what was, what was it like writing on friends and then writing on how much your mother were you in your head about like, I want to, did you have a voice in your head saying, I know I want to steer things away from things we did at friends.
那个，我感觉我得问一个显而易见的问题，在《老友记》和《老爸老妈浪漫史》里分别担任编剧，是一种什么样的体验？在你脑海里，有没有想过“我一定要……”你脑子里有没有一个声音在说，“我知道我想刻意避开我们在《老友记》里做过的事情”？

00:16:07.039 --> 00:16:12.179
I think sometimes you helped us do that when we were inadvertently stepping on some storyline and you'd go, I don't know about that.
我觉得有时候当我们无意间踩到类似的剧情线时，你会帮我们避免，你会说：“这个我就不知道了。”

00:16:12.179 --> 00:16:17.600
What was your own, what did you, what was your feeling of that journey of having been on both shows?
你自己的感受是怎样的？经历过这两部剧之后，你对这段历程有什么感想？

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:22.539
Um, you know, friends was the ultimate learning experience for me.
嗯，你知道的，《老友记》对我的写作是一次终极的学习体验。

00:16:22.539 --> 00:16:30.500
You know, and I learned from the best and we put in whatever 18 hours a day for six, seven years, whatever it was that I was on that.
你知道的，我是向最优秀的人学习的。我们在那里的六七年里，每天不管工作18个小时还是多久，反正我就是这么度过的。

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:34.379
And I learned from David Crane and Murdock Hoffman, who are just brilliant.
我向大卫·克兰（David Crane）和莫塔·霍夫曼（Marta Kauffman）学习，他们简直才华横溢。

00:16:34.379 --> 00:16:41.059
And I was the same age as, you know, a lot of the staff was the same age as the actors, as the characters.
而且我当时的年龄，你知道的，剧组很多工作人员和演员、角色都是同龄人。

00:16:41.059 --> 00:16:43.860
And so every, I mean, everything in my life went into that show.
所以，我生活中的一切都融入到了那部剧里。

