That thing of like people from another country, and that wasn't my joke in any way, but sure was Stephen Lloyd.
那种来自另一个国家的人的反应，虽然这绝不是我写的笑话，但斯蒂芬·劳埃德（Stephen Lloyd）绝对是。

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:44.619
But that kind of joke just really makes me laugh.
但那种笑话真的让我捧腹大笑。

00:21:44.619 --> 00:21:50.099
Kobe always played the exasperation at not having the same cultural references, like very well.
科比（Kobe）总是把那种因没有相同文化共鸣而产生的恼怒，演得非常到位。

00:21:50.099 --> 00:21:50.940
Like, just.
就像，简直了。

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:52.380
Yeah, cause that was real.
对，因为那是真实的。

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:54.140
And she was like, wow, they do more Canada.
她当时就像是，哇，他们真够加拿大的。

00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:57.299
That was an actor playing the reality.
那是一个演员在演绎真实的生活。

00:21:57.299 --> 00:22:34.460
Greg, I thought you were gonna say one of the earliest things you said to us what that made me that made me it was when we met with you about you maybe working on the show and I go this is the guy you said I love that you have a couple that loves each other on this show you have a couple that's not the bicker sins it's not like the typical TV couple and it's the sort of heavy said guy and the wife who's taking the piss out of him is like they actually really like each other and it's funny because you join then you join the show and they were apart and And I think that's why you wanted to write the episode that got them back together, isn't it?
格雷格，我还以为你要说你对我们说过的最早的一番话呢，那让我——那让我——是在我们跟你见面聊你可能加入这个节目的时候，我说：“这就是我们要找的人！”你当时说：“我喜欢这个节目里有一对相爱的情侣，你们有一对不爱吵嘴的情侣，他们不像是典型的电视剧情侣，而且那种刻板的硬汉形象，加上妻子总是拿他开涮，就好像他们其实真的很喜欢对方一样。”这很有趣，因为后来你加入了，结果他们分开了，而我想这就是为什么你想写让他们复合的那集，对吧？

00:22:34.460 --> 00:22:55.339
Because that was one of the things we talked about at Will and Grits, and one of the things that I thought was the most challenging and something that I wanted to learn, because I took it a step further than what you're saying, that yes, there was a couple of them there that loved each other, and clearly were meant to be each other, yet they were funny, they were really funny in love.
因为那是我们在威尔和格里茨（Will and Grits）讨论的事情之一，而且我认为这是最具挑战性的事情之一，也是我想去学习的，因为我比你刚才说的更进了一步，是的，那里确实有一对情侣他们彼此相爱，并且很明显是天生一对，但他们很有趣，他们在爱中真的非常有趣。

00:22:55.339 --> 00:23:03.980
And if you remember on Friends, we got Ross and Rachel together and then broke them up as fast as we possibly could because they weren't nearly as funny together as they were apart.
如果你还记得《老友记》（Friends），我们让罗斯（Ross）和瑞秋（Rachel）在一起了，然后又以最快的速度让他们分手，因为他们在一起时根本没有分开时那么有趣。

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:04.819
Right.
对。

00:23:04.819 --> 00:23:09.450
So to see a couple that is funny together was a revelation to me.
所以看到一对在一起很有趣的情侣对我来说是个启示。

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:12.329
And I was like, I gotta learn how to do that.
当时我就想，我必须学会怎么写那样的内容。

00:23:12.329 --> 00:23:13.569
Was it a bait and switch though?
但这算不算是虚假宣传呢？

00:23:13.569 --> 00:23:16.450
Did you sign up to wanna be on the show and then we broke them up?
你加入节目是想参与他们在一起的剧情，结果我们却让他们分手了？

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:18.690
Did Eric had me broken them up already?
难道埃里克（Eric）在我加入之前就已经让他们分手了吗？

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:20.490
No, they broke up at the end of season one.
不，他们在第一季末分手的。

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:21.329
So they went.
所以他们去了。

00:23:21.329 --> 00:23:23.210
I know, but we met Greg during season one.
我知道，但我们是在第一季期间认识格雷格的。

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:24.130
I think didn't we, Greg?
难道不是吗，格雷格？

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:26.049
Didn't we meet you as it was airing anyway?
我们不就是在节目刚播出时认识你的吗？

00:23:26.049 --> 00:23:27.450
I might be misremembering.
也许我记错了。

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:30.089
But you wanted to get them back together, which I love.
但你想让他们复合，我太喜欢了。

00:23:30.089 --> 00:23:32.890
You're talking about the fancy lunch at the commissary, right?
你说的是在员工餐厅吃的那顿高级午餐，对吧？

00:23:32.890 --> 00:23:33.890
We had a fancy commissary.
我们当时确实有个高级员工餐厅。

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:34.890
I remember that.
我记得那个。

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:35.890
Yeah.
对。

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:36.890
You see.
你看。

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:37.890
I mean, we were nervous.
我的意思是，我们当时很紧张。

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:38.890
We were intimidated.
我们很敬畏。

00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:39.890
We were, I want to make clear.
我们，我想说明白这一点。

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:42.089
Carter and I are both huge friends fans.
我和卡特（Carter）都是《老友记》的超级粉丝。

00:23:42.089 --> 00:23:44.690
Like, whatever that cold open was or wasn't to side.
不管那个冷开场是不是在致敬，暂且不论。

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:47.690
We love friends and we were very intimidated by you.
我们深爱着《老友记》，而且我们对你感到非常敬畏。

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:53.809
And we, you know, there was still the feeling from the studio on the network that these two guys, Carter and Craig, are very young.
而且你知道的，电视网和制片厂当时依然有一种看法，觉得卡特和格雷格这两个家伙太年轻了。

00:23:53.809 --> 00:23:55.089
They've never run a TV show.
他们从来没有运作过一档电视剧。

00:23:55.089 --> 00:23:58.410
They never had, they never staffed on this real sitcom.
他们从来没有、从来没有在真正的处境喜剧里担任过编剧。

00:23:58.410 --> 00:24:08.009
So the confidence it gave them for you to join was very real, but then you came in and you were great and you wanted to keep making, help us make this show, not make it more like another show.
所以，你的加入给他们带来的信心是实实在在的，但接着你来了，你表现得棒极了，你想要继续创作，帮助我们把这个节目做好，而不是把它变得像另一部剧。

